---
permalink: /feed.xml
eleventyExcludeFromCollections: true
site_url: "https://haraldrevery.com"
---
{#-
  RSS 2.0 feed for the whole site, at /feed.xml.

  SCOPE: Notebook posts AND music releases, interleaved newest-first. One feed
  rather than two so there is a single URL to subscribe to; the merge and the
  sort happen in the `feedItems` filter (eleventy.config.js), because Nunjucks
  cannot concatenate two collections and re-sort them, and the two shapes differ
  (a post is an Eleventy item, a release is a plain object off input_release/).
  Every item carries a <category> of "notebook" or "release" so a subscriber can
  filter one out without unsubscribing from both.

  RSS 2.0 rather than Atom purely for reach: every reader understands it.
  <atom:link rel="self"> is the one Atom element RSS validators require.

  DATES: <pubDate> is RFC-822 (rfc822Date filter), NOT the ISO format the
  sitemap uses. Release dates come in as `dateUTC`, never the raw JSON string --
  unpadded strings like "2019-5-14" parse as LOCAL midnight and would publish
  the previous day west of GMT (see the releases collection). Readers dedupe on
  <guid>, so guid must stay the page's permanent clean URL -- changing a slug
  creates a NEW entry in every subscriber's reader, which is why guid is the URL
  and not a hash.

  <lastBuildDate> is the newest ITEM date, never the build time: a feed whose
  channel date moves on every rebuild makes readers re-poll for nothing (same
  reasoning as <lastmod> in sitemap.njk).

  Description-only, no full text: posts from input_custom_html_pages/ are
  complete standalone documents copied verbatim (see the eleventy.before hook),
  so they have no templateContent to inline. A feed where half the entries carry
  full text and half carry none is worse than one that is consistently a teaser.

  DISCOVERY: eleventy_settings/base.njk emits the <link rel="alternate"> for
  every page built on it; discography.njk and release.njk are standalone
  documents and carry their own copy of that tag.
-#}
{%- set items = collections.notebook_posts | feedItems(collections.releases) -%}
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom">
  <channel>
    <title>Harald Revery</title>
    <link>{{ site_url }}/</link>
    <description>Music releases, personal notes, essays and interactive experiments on music, mathematics, physics and photography.</description>
    <language>en-us</language>
    <copyright>Harald Revery</copyright>
    <lastBuildDate>{{ items | newestDate | rfc822Date }}</lastBuildDate>
    <atom:link href="{{ site_url }}/feed.xml" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml" />
    {# RSS 2.0 caps <image> at 144x400. opengraphimg.jpg is 1200x630 and trips
       that check in the W3C feed validator, so the channel icon is the square
       96px favicon instead. #}
    <image>
      <url>{{ site_url }}/favicon-96x96.png</url>
      <title>Harald Revery</title>
      <link>{{ site_url }}/</link>
      <width>96</width>
      <height>96</height>
    </image>
{%- for item in items %}
    <item>
      <title>{{ item.title }}</title>
      <link>{{ site_url }}{{ item.url }}</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">{{ site_url }}{{ item.url }}</guid>
      <pubDate>{{ item.date | rfc822Date }}</pubDate>
      {%- if item.description %}
      <description>{{ item.description }}</description>
      {%- endif %}
      <category>{{ item.kind }}</category>
      {%- for category in item.categories %}
      <category>{{ category }}</category>
      {%- endfor %}
      {#- Artwork as an <enclosure>: the one element RSS 2.0 gives for attaching
          media, and what readers use to show a thumbnail next to an entry.
          Cover art for a release, the post's own og:image for a notebook entry.
          RSS allows at most one enclosure per item, so this stays one image. -#}
      {%- if item.image %}
      <enclosure url="{{ site_url }}{{ item.image }}" length="{{ item.image | fileSize }}" type="{{ item.image | mimeType }}" />
      {%- endif %}
    </item>
{%- endfor %}
  </channel>
</rss>
